				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>Rule_Title</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>The rsyslog service must be running in RHEL 8.</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>Vuln_Discuss</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>Configuring RHEL 8 to implement organization-wide security implementation guides and security checklists ensures compliance with federal standards.</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>IA_Controls</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA />
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>Check_Content</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>Verify that "rsyslog" is being used to monitor auditing on the system.

Check that the "rsyslog" service is enabled and active with the following commands:

$ sudo systemctl is-enabled rsyslog
enabled

$ sudo systemctl is-active rsyslog
active

If the service is not enabled and active, this is a finding.</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>Fix_Text</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>Start the "rsyslog" service and enable it to start at boot with the following commands:

$ sudo systemctl enable --now rsyslog</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
